WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.600
A letter waits on a writing desk.

2
00:00:02.650 --> 00:00:04.460
Sir, it begins.

3
00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:07.780
I am exceedingly obliged
by your communication.

4
00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:11.370
Then the sentence keeps going,
through comma after comma,

5
00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:12.830
until you've lost where it started.

6
00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:15.260
And what does inst mean?

7
00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:17.500
Or earnest, here?

8
00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:23.040
Under exam pressure, how do you
crack a letter written 170 years ago?

9
00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:27.340
In this mission, you'll meet the
four bumps in 19th-century prose:

10
00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:32.080
vocabulary, long sentences,
formal register and context.

11
00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:35.060
Translate every word into
modern English first?

12
00:00:35.110 --> 00:00:36.950
Skip the long sentences?

13
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:39.900
You'll test what really
works against the clock.

14
00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:42.800
And you'll take on a
real Victorian letter,

15
00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:45.210
one tangled sentence at a time.

16
00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:48.920
Your mission: work out how
to crack a letter this old.
